the search stage, and the design contrast is confounded with table size, participant count and era, so the evidence supports a bounded descriptive claim and not a causal claim about evaluation designs or a claim of a better search method.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The grounded artifacts contain one completed sealed final evaluation (outcome qualified, status completed, correct true) of a frozen selected configuration, plus a three-attempt search stage that found no winner (all three scored 0.4993 on search data, equal to the starting baseline, search delta 0.0; 3 of 3 attempts scored, 0 unscored). The strongest honest paper is therefore a measurement study of leaderboard fragility under one precisely defined perturbation (deleting comparisons), with the search procedure framed as the measurement instrument, evaluator-owned baselines (random removal, first-order influence only, opponent-targeted removal) as comparators, and the evaluator's empty-set, label-permutation and cross-table copy controls as validity checks. This beats a method-superiority story (not supported: selected equals influence-only baseline on all four real final cases and search delta is zero) and beats a run-diary story (three tied variants are not a result). Qualification is reported as achieved; it certifies the evaluation protocol completed and its controls passed, not that the method is better or that findings generalise. The 'sensitivity is small / depends on conditions' outcome is explicitly acceptable per the task, and the search-stage expert-judgment table that could not be flipped within the cap supplies a genuine, bounded 'unexpected' finding with unknown cause.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Edit mode is not active: no previous_paper directory or edit-context files exist in the workspace, so there is no prior manuscript to preserve, broaden or recenter. The status 'rejected' is used only to mean that no prior story is carried forward; the framing is a fresh thesis-first measurement paper.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior manuscript or new-program inputs exist; the framing rests only on paper_data.json (final_evaluation, downstream_study, grounding_sources), the staged task/evaluator files and the selected program source under source_evidence/downstream.\",\n    \"The selected program (source attempt c01) is the frozen studied method; the starting baseline program_0 has a search score equal to it (0.4993, delta 0.0). It was not run in the sealed final stage, so no final-stage baseline-versus-selected program comparison is available and none may be written; the final-stage comparators are only the evaluator-owned B1 to B3 reference procedures.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Refit-verified influence-guided removal search (studied configuration)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the frozen selected configuration and the measurement instrument: Bradley-Terry fit by damped Newton, per-row first-order influence on the gap theta_hi minus theta_lo with leave-one-out leverage correction, re-linearised greedy batches, refit-verified prefix bisection, drop-and-readd polish, and acceptance only after the evaluator-equivalent exact refit flips the pair. Present it as an upper bound on the minimal budget (any verified flip is a valid certificate; minimality is not proven).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Random removal (B1)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Evaluator-owned reference: 20 random orderings of the first k_cap rows with prefix bisection per case. Shows that undirected deletion rarely flips: 0/20 draws in F1, F3, F4 and 6/20 in F2 (mean score 0.164); establishes that tiny budgets come from direction, not from fragility to arbitrary deletion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"First-order influence only with exact-refit bisection (B2)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Evaluator-owned reference and the decisive honest comparator: its verified budgets equal the studied configuration on all four real final cases (62, 4, 62, 8 rows) and on both permutation controls (2, 10 rows); the reference influence score 0.9261 equals the final mean score 0.9261. The paper must state that the added search stages gave no measured gain on the final cases.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Opponent-targeted removal (B3)\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Evaluator-owned heuristic that deletes the upper-ranked model's wins over the strongest opponents; needs 650, 6, 88, 12 rows in F1 to F4. Shows a plausible hand-built attack needs more rows than the influence ordering in all four real cases (650 vs 62, 6 vs 4, 88 vs 62, 12 vs 8).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Validity controls: empty set, label permutation, cross-table copy\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Final evidence reports empty-set removal does not flip any case, permuted-outcome tables are non-vacuous (moved fraction 0.660 vs expected 0.662 on the crowd table, 0.498 vs 0.500 on the sports table; rating-spread ratio 0.088 and 0.160), and applying a removal set from one design's table to the other flips 0 of 2 trials. Permuted tables flip with 2 and 10 rows, giving a control gap of +0.112 (permuted minus real mean score), which supports reading real-data budgets as reflecting real rating gaps.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Search-stage variants (cell-aggregated relaxed search; stationarity LP)\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Two further variants, both tied at search score 0.4993 with the first, with per-episode runtimes of about 2 s and about 114 s versus about 1.3 s. Report compactly as a negative result about search sophistication on the search data (and in the supplement), not as a chronology. Do not claim they were evaluated on final data.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Final-stage main view (sealed_final, grounded in final.evidence.episodes and final.metric.*): verified removal budget k per case for crowd top (F1, k=62 of 135,630 rows, cap 2,713), crowd mid (F2, k=4), sports top (F3, k=62 of 12,900 rows, cap 258), sports mid (F4, k=8), expressed both as rows and as percent of all comparisons, with B1 flip counts and B2/B3 budgets alongside. Both final episodes (seeds 21, 22) returned identical per-case budgets, so report zero observed episode-to-episode variation and state that this is not a population-level interval over datasets.\",\n    \"Design contrast: family means of the cap-normalised budget-saving score are 0.988 (crowd) and 0.864 (sports), combined 0.926; mean budget as fraction of cap 0.074. Interpret the contrast as a descriptive difference between two specific tables; list confounds (rows 135,630 vs 12,900; 52 models vs 30 teams; era; vote granularity; ties handling) and do not attribute it to evaluation design per se.\",\n    \"Top versus mid pairs: mid-table pairs are cheaper in both designs (4 vs 62; 8 vs 62 rows), consistent with smaller rating gaps; do not claim a mechanism beyond what the first-order influence formula and permutation control support.\",\n    \"Controls and validity: empty-set no flip; permutation controls non-vacuous and flip with 2 and 10 rows; copy-control 0/2; control gap +0.112. Explain what each control rules out (trivially flipping targets, vacuous permutations, row-index leakage across designs).\",\n    \"Method-ablation-style negative result: selected configuration equals influence-only B2 on every final case; search-stage tie of three variants (0.4993 each, delta 0.0, 3 of 3 scored). Keep search and sealed-final numbers in separate sections and tables and never merge their scores.\",\n    \"Search-stage expert-judgment finding: on the 6-model MT-Bench human table the search-stage evaluator reports expert-family score 0.0 and flip rate 0.5 over four real cases, which (with crowd family score 0.9987) implies neither expert target flipped within the 2% cap; the cause (myopic search vs true infeasibility under the cap) is unknown and must be labelled unknown. No expert-design result exists in the final stage, so any design conclusion for expert judgment is search-stage and limited.\",\n    \"Scope statement: point-estimate order reversal of one adjacent pair per case; cap 2% of comparisons; ridge 0.01; ties as half wins; no confidence-interval-based rank events, insertion, label flipping or vote rigging; no model-judge (LLM judge) design evaluated; two final designs only.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1 (overview, early, page 1 or 2): protocol schematic from comparison table through Bradley-Terry refit, target adjacent pair, removal search, exact-refit verification to budget k and score 1 - k/k_cap, with the baselines B1 to B3 and controls as side branches and the two evaluated designs marked, search-stage versus final-stage data labelled.\",\n    \"Figure 2 (main result): two-panel grouped bar chart, one panel per design (crowd votes; NBA games), x categories top pair and mid pair, series studied configuration, first-order influence only (B2), opponent-targeted (B3), with log-scale y-axis of removal budget as percent of all comparisons and the 2% cap drawn as a reference line; random-removal outcome annotated as flips out of 20 draws.\",\n    \"Figure 3 (controls): two panels, (a) verified budget on real versus label-permuted tables per design, (b) rating-spread ratio and moved fraction versus expected for the permuted tables; supports the interpretation that budgets track rating structure.\",\n    \"Tables rather than figures for the dataset/protocol summary (rows, models, cap, search vs final role), per-case budget table with B1 to B3, and the search-stage variant table (score, family scores, per-episode runtime).\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"protocol_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Give the reader an immediate picture of the studied perturbation (delete comparisons, refit exactly, test whether an adjacent pair reverses), the removal-search instrument, baselines, controls, and which two designs were measured in the sealed final stage versus the search stage.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",\n      \"metric\": \"none (conceptual)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"ICLR 2026 template is single-column with 5.5 in text width; a left-to-right pipeline with 5 to 6 boxes and short labels needs the full text width to keep font at least 7 pt; place on page 1 or 2.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"main_budget_by_case\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show the verified minimal removal budget for the four real final cases and the evaluator-owned comparators, making the crowd versus sports contrast, the top versus mid contrast, and the studied-equals-B2 result visible together.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_bar\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"case (top pair, mid pair)\",\n      \"metric\": \"verified removal budget as percent of all comparisons (log scale), k from final.evidence.episodes\",\n      \"split_by\": \"design (crowd votes, sports games)\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Two side-by-side panels with 2 categories and 3 series each, log axis and a cap line; at 5.5 in total width each panel is about 2.6 in, enough for legible 8 pt labels. Draw studied and B2 bars distinguishable (hatch/offset) since they coinci