": \"LLM usage disclosure and limitations checklist\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"ICLR-required LLM Usage statement (the research search and code were produced with LLM assistance as part of an automated research pipeline; writer should state this plainly and anonymously), plus extended limitations: four real final cases, two designs, two identical episodes, no confidence-interval events, no rigging or adversarial insertion, no model-judge design in final.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"study.investigation_outcome\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"forbidden_headings\": [\n    \"Experiment 1 / Experiment 2\",\n    \"Campaign Overview\",\n    \"Search Progression\",\n    \"Milestones\",\n    \"First Valid Result\",\n    \"Iteration History\",\n    \"Attempt Chronology\",\n    \"What We Tried\",\n    \"Lessons Learned from the Run\",\n    \"Pipeline Details\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_phrases\": [\n    \"correctness rate\",\n    \"correct and eligible\",\n    \"eligible\",\n    \"combined_score\",\n    \"rubric\",\n    \"worker_01\",\n    \"state-of-the-art\",\n    \"outperforms the baseline\",\n    \"significantly better\",\n    \"we discovered that leaderboards are fragile\",\n    \"proves that the minimum is\",\n    \"guarantees minimality\",\n    \"design X causes\",\n    \"after several iterations\",\n    \"in the first cycle\",\n    \"the campaign\",\n    \"program_1\",\n    \"idea_id\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_writer\": [\n    \"Honest headline: the studied configuration is not shown to beat the influence-only baseline (equal budgets on all four real final cases; search delta 0.0). Make the contribution the measurement and its bounded findings, not a new algorithm. Qualified final outcome means the sealed evaluation completed with controls passing; say exactly that and do not read it as superiority, validity beyond the declared cases, or generalisation.\",\n    \"Search and sealed-final results are different evaluation scopes: keep separate sections/tables, never average or compare their scores directly, and note the data differ (arena-55k and MT-Bench vs arena-140k dedup and NBA).\",\n    \"Reader-facing vocabulary: 'removal budget', 'budget-saving score (1 - k/cap)', 'verified flip', 'seeded run', 'model variant', 'configuration'. Never show combined_score, eligible, correct, rubric or worker names. If a thresholded rate is needed use 'flip success fraction' (e.g. random removal flipped 0 of 20 draws) with one meaning per figure.\",\n    \"Report budgets in two units: rows removed and percent of all comparisons. Note that the evaluator's 'budget fraction' metric (mean 0.074) is k divided by the cap, i.e. a fraction of the 2% allowance, not of all comparisons; the percent-of-comparisons numbers are derived by dividing k by n_rows (e.g. 62/135,630) and should be labelled as derived.\",\n    \"Uncertainty: only two final episodes (seeds 21, 22), identical outputs, 20 random draws for B1. Say that no episode-to-episode variation was observed, that this cannot support significance claims, and that case-level results are single-table descriptive outcomes. The verified budget is an upper bound on the true minimum; exact minimality is not established.\",\n    \"Confounds in the design contrast: table size (135,630 vs 12,900 rows), participants (52 models vs 30 teams), era, outcome granularity, tie rates and match structure all differ between tables; evaluation design is not isolated. Do not generalise to expert annotation or model-judge designs from the final stage; for expert judgment, only the search-stage MT-Bench non-flip within cap can be cited, with unknown cause.\",\n    \"The task brief's stated required evidence is at least two evaluation designs of real data: the final stage supplies crowd votes and sports; the search stage adds expert judgment. State this and the datasets' versions from the task brief; the deduplication numbers come from the staged final metadata.\",\n    \"Citations: only keys present in references.bib may be cited. The bib contains Bradley-Terry-model literature and Broderick2020AnAF (data-dropping robustness metric) but does not contain Chatbot Arena, MT-Bench, influence functions, vote rigging or leaderboard-illusion entries; do not invent bib keys. Describe those only with sources actually present in the bib or in the task brief, and flag the gap in the writer's own checks rather than fabricating entries. Do not retrieve, read or cite the withheld prior paper.\",\n    \"Figure 1 should use generate-concept-figure; it is the only planned figure with that generator. Figures 2 and 3 use matplotlib, exported to PDF and PNG at 300 DPI; reference the PDF in LaTeX; if only raster exists it must be at least 2400 px wide for full-width and regenerated, not upscaled. All three figures are full text width (5.5 in) in the single-column ICLR template; do not shrink a dense figure to fit.\",\n    \"Body-page plan (8 to 9 pages, last page at least 75% full, no padding): introduction with research question and bounded contribution list (1.0 page); related work on Bradley-Terry fitting, data-dropping robustness (Broderick et al.), and leaderboard manipulation context limited to what is citable (0.6); problem and method with formal flip-budget definition, instrument, baselines, controls (2.0 including Figure 1); experimental protocol, datasets table, cases, metrics (1.0); results with Figure 2, per-case table, Figure 3, search-stage tie as a short negative-result subsection and expert-table non-flip (2.5); discussion, scope conditions and limitations that a reviewer would raise (1.0). Spend any remaining space on clearer definitions and limitation analysis, not on additional claims.\",\n    \"Include the ICLR-required LLM usage statement and keep the manuscript anonymous: no repository links, no author or affiliation text.\",\n    \"Do not narrate the order in which variants were tried; present the three search variants as 'three search configurations' in a single comparison table with identical-score outcome.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}@Article{Davidson1970OnET,\n author = {R. Davidson},\n journal = {Journal of the American Statistical Association},\n pages = {317-328},\n title = {On Extending the Bradley-Terry Model to Accommodate Ties in Paired Comparison Experiments},\n volume = {65},\n year = {1970}\n}\n\n@Article{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,\n author = {G. Simons and Yi-Ching Yao},\n journal = {Annals of Statistics},\n pages = {1041-1060},\n title = {Asymptotics when the number of parameters tends to infinity in the Bradley-Terry model for paired comparisons},\n volume = {27},\n year = {1999}\n}\n\n@Article{Davidson1977OnET,\n author = {R. Davidson and R. Beaver},\n journal = {Biometrics},\n pages = {693},\n title = {On Extending the Bradley-Terry Model to Incorporate Within-Pair Order Effects},\n volume = {33},\n year = {1977}\n}\n\n@Article{Gao2021UncertaintyQI,\n author = {Chao Gao and Yandi Shen and A. Y. Zhang},\n booktitle = {Information and Inference A Journal of the IMA},\n journal = {Information and Inference: A Journal of the IMA},\n title = {Uncertainty quantification in the Bradley–Terry–Luce model},\n year = {2021}\n}\n\n@Article{Leonard1977AnAB,\n author = {Tom Leonard},\n journal = {Biometrics},\n pages = {121},\n title = {An Alternative Bayesian Approach to the Bradley-Terry Model for Paired Comparisons},\n volume = {33},\n year = {1977}\n}\n\n@Article{Davidson1969MultivariatePC,\n author = {R. Davidson and R. Bradley},\n journal = {Biometrika},\n pages = {81-95},\n title = {Multivariate paired comparisons: The extension of a univariate model and associated estimation and test procedures},\n volume = {56},\n year = {1969}\n}\n\n@Article{Yan2011GROUPEDSP,\n author = {T. Yan and Yaning Yang and Jin-Feng Xu},\n journal = {arXiv: Other Statistics},\n title = {GROUPED SPARSE PAIRED COMPARISONS IN THE BRADLEY-TERRY MODEL},\n year = {2011}\n}\n\n@Article{Causeur2005A2E,\n author = {D. Causeur and F. Husson},\n journal = {Journal of Statistical Planning and Inference},\n pages = {245-259},\n title = {A 2-dimensional extension of the Bradley–Terry model for paired comparisons},\n volume = {135},\n year = {2005}\n}\n\n@Article{Bong2021GeneralizedRF,\n author = {Heejong Bong and A. Rinaldo},\n booktitle = {International Conference on Machine Learning},\n pages = {2160-2177},\n title = {Generalized Results for the Existence and Consistency of the MLE in the Bradley-Terry-Luce Model},\n year = {2021}\n}\n\n@Article{Stern2011ModeratedPC,\n author = {Steven E. Stern},\n journal = {Journal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series C (Applied Statistics)},\n title = {Moderated paired comparisons: a generalized Bradley–Terry model for continuous data using a discontinuous penalized likelihood function},\n volume = {60},\n year = {2011}\n}\n\n@Article{Fageot2023GeneralizedBM,\n author = {J. Fageot and Sadegh Farhadkhani and Lê-Nguyên Hoang and Oscar Villemaud},\n booktitle = {AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence},\n pages = {20379-20386},\n title = {Generalized Bradley-Terry Models for Score Estimation from Paired Comparisons},\n year = {2023}\n}\n\n@Article{Genest1999DerivingPF,\n author = {C. Genest and C. E. M'lan},\n journal = {Mathematical and Computer Modelling},\n pages = {87-102},\n title = {Deriving priorities from the Bradley-Terry model},\n volume = {29},\n year = {1999}\n}\n\n@Article{Butler2004TheEO,\n author = {K. Butler and J. Whelan},\n journal = {arXiv: Statistics Theory},\n title = {The existence of maximum likelihood estimates in the Bradley-Terry model and its extensions},\n year = {2004}\n}\n\n@Article{Makur2024MinimaxHT,\n author = {A. Makur and Japneet Singh},\n booktitle = {IEEE Transactions on Information Theory},\n journal = {IEEE Transactions on Information Theory},\n pages = {9163-9202},\n title = {Minimax Hypothesis Testing for the Bradley–Terry–Luce Model},\n volume = {71},\n year = {2024}\n}\n\n@Article{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP,\n au