{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014tcWGj3TbKJhrtQrAuvCB3","type":"tool_result","content":"39:Public leaderboards built from pairwise comparisons fit a Bradley--Terry model and publish a ranking, yet how many comparisons must change before a ranking reverses is rarely measured. We study the smallest set of deleted comparisons, within a cap of 2\\% of all rows, that reverses the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair after an exact ridge-regularised Bradley--Terry refit. We measure it on a crowd-vote table (Chatbot Arena, 135,630 comparisons) and an NBA game table (12,900 comparisons). Verified budgets are small: 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of the crowd table (at most 0.046\\% of comparisons) and 62 and 8 rows for the sports table (0.48\\% and 0.062\\%). Mid-table pairs are cheaper than top pairs, and random deletion flips the top pairs in 0 of 20 draws. However, a static first-order influence ranking with refit bisection reaches every one of these budgets, so the more elaborate search we studied adds no measurable gain, and the crowd-versus-sports contrast is confounded with table size, participants and era. A six-model expert-judgment table did not flip within the cap in development runs, for an unknown reason. All findings are descriptive: four cases, two designs, one rating model.\n49:\\paragraph{What we do.} We measure verified removal budgets on two held-out tables of real data: a deduplicated Chatbot Arena crowd-vote table and an NBA game-results table. We compare the studied search configuration, which orders rows by first-order influence on the rating gap and then refines the set with re-linearised greedy batches and a local polish, against three evaluator-owned comparators (random removal, first-order influence only, and opponent-targeted removal) and three validity controls (empty set, label permutation, cross-table copy). A separate pair of development tables, including a six-model expert-judgment table, is used to compare search configurations and is reported separately.\n53:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (1) A precisely specified, evaluator-verified removal-budget protocol with comparators and controls, whose budgets are certificates rather than estimates. (2) Measured budgets for four held-out cases on two evaluation designs, with the crowd-versus-sports contrast and its confounds spelled out. (3) A negative result: first-order influence ordering with exact-refit bisection matches the more elaborate configuration everywhere we measured. (4) An explicit scope statement: the claims concern the declared cases only, not confidence-interval-based rank changes, adversarial insertion or vote rigging, other rating models, or causal effects of evaluation design.\n85:where $p_i=\\sigma(d_i)$, $w_i=p_i(1-p_i)$, $H$ is the Hessian of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} (including the ridge), and $q_i=x_i^\\top H^{-1}x_i$ with $x_i=e_{a_i}-e_{b_i}$ gives the leave-one-out leverage correction. Rows with the most negative $\\Delta_i$ close the gap fastest to first order. The expectation is that removing the most negative rows first needs far fewer deletions than undirected removal. Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} is a linearisation, so the question we test is twofold: whether an influence order is enough to reach the exact-refit budget, and whether re-linearising and polishing it reduces the budget further.\n91:Three comparators are computed by the evaluator on the same tables and scored with the same rule. \\textbf{B1, random removal:} 20 random row orders, each truncated to $\\kcap$ rows and bisected over prefixes; we report the number of draws that flip. \\textbf{B2, influence only:} one static ranking by the uncorrected first-order influence at the full-table fit, followed by exact-refit prefix bisection; this is step (a) without leverage correction, re-linearisation or polishing. \\textbf{B3, opponent-targeted removal:} delete rows in which the upper-ranked participant beat another participant, ordered by the opponent's rating from strongest downwards, then bisect over prefixes; this is a plausible hand-built attack that needs no gradient.\n172:So the re-linearisation, the leverage correction and the polish gave no measured reduction on these cases. The same holds on development tables (Section~\\ref{sec:dev}). A single static ranking by first-order influence followed by exact-refit bisection is therefore enough to reach every budget we report, and the budgets can be viewed as properties of that simple procedure's output rather than of the more elaborate one. Opponent-targeted removal needs more rows in all four cases: 650 versus 62, 6 versus 4, 88 versus 62 and 12 versus 8. The gap is large only for the crowd top pair (10.5 times), and modest elsewhere (1.4 to 1.5 times). Random removal's mean overall score is 0.041, against 0.926 for B2. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n174:We did not run the starting baseline on the held-out tables. By inspection of its source, its first strategy is the same static influence ranking with prefix bisection as B2 and its second strategy runs only when the first finds no flip within the cap; we therefore expect, but have not measured, that it would reproduce B2's budgets here.\n189:Table~\\ref{tab:dev} reports the development evaluation of three search configurations: the influence-guided configuration studied above, a configuration that aggregates rows into cells and solves a relaxed removal problem with exact swap polishing, and one that solves a stationarity linear program over target ratings. All three scored 0.4993, the same as the starting baseline (difference 0.0); all three were scored (3 of 3, none missing). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study]]\n203:Stationarity linear program & 0.4993 & 0.9987 & 0.0 & 0.5 & 114\\\\\n225:We measured how few comparisons must be deleted to reverse the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair in a Bradley--Terry leaderboard, using exact-refit verification, comparators and controls. On two held-out tables of real data, the verified budgets were 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 135,630-row crowd-vote table and 62 and 8 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 12,900-row sports table. Mid pairs were cheaper than top pairs, random deletion rarely helped, and label-permuted tables flipped with far fewer rows than real ones. A static first-order influence ranking matched the more elaborate search everywhere, and three search configurations tied on development tables, so we claim a measurement and not a better method. The crowd-versus-sports difference in percent of comparisons is mostly table size and is confounded with participants and era. An expert-judgment table on the development side did not flip within the cap, with unknown cause. Next steps that the evidence motivates are more tables per design, rank-interval events, exhaustive or exact solvers on small tables to test how far the certified budgets are from the true minimum, and adversarial perturbations beyond deletion.\n269:The Newton fit uses analytic gradient $\\nabla L=\\sum_i (p_i-s_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})+\\lambda\\theta$ and Hessian $H=\\sum_i w_i\\,x_ix_i^\\top+\\lambda I$, with step halving until the objective stops increasing (at most 60 iterations, gradient tolerance $10^{-9}$). Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl} floors the leverage denominator $1-w_iq_i$ at $10^{-3}$. Step (b) uses a shrink factor $0.6$ and stops when the removed set reaches the current size limit. Step (c) begins at a drop fraction of $0.25$ and halves it after each failure. All fits are warm-started from the previous solution. The evaluator's fit and the candidate's fit use the same objective; the evaluator's acceptance test is $\\theta_{\\hi}<\\theta_{\\lo}$ with no margin. The row table given to the search procedure is anonymised and shuffled, so neither model names nor time order are available to it. Source: the selected candidate's \\texttt{src/program.py} in the staged evidence. A further file in that directory (\\texttt{datasets\\_offline.py}) holds loaders for offline analysis, was not imported by the evaluated code, and was not executed; we make no claim from it.\nfonts/type1/urw/helvetic/uhvb8a.pfb></usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/fonts/type1/\nurw/times/utmb8a.pfb></usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/fonts/type1/urw/times/utmr8\na.pfb></usr/share/texlive/texmf-dist/fonts/type1/urw/times/utmri8a.pfb>\nOutput written on paper.pdf (13 pages, 283019 bytes).\nTranscript written on paper.log.\n545:Overfull \\hbox (34.83162pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 279--289","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"69025aa5-9399-474f-82bf-b38cf111820f","uuid":"a872beb0-3b05-442b-88e7-9780b4f13eab","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:05:56.933Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"39:Public leaderboards built from pairwise comparisons fit a Bradley--Terry model and publish a ranking, yet how many comparisons must change before a ranking reverses is rarely measured. We study the smallest set of deleted comparisons, within a cap of 2\\% of all rows, that reverses the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair after an exact ridge-regularised Bradley--Terry refit. We measure it on a crowd-vote table (Chatbot Arena, 135,630 comparisons) and an NBA game table (12,900 comparisons). Verified budgets are small: 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of the crowd table (at most 0.046\\% of comparisons) and 62 and 8 rows for the sports table (0.48\\% and 0.062\\%). Mid-table pairs are cheaper than top pairs, and random deletion flips the top pairs in 0 of 20 draws. However, a static first-order influence ranking with refit bisection reaches every one of these budgets, so the more elaborate search we studied adds no measurable gain, and the crowd-versus-sports contrast is confounded with table size, participants and era. A six-model expert-judgment table did not flip within the cap in development runs, for an unknown reason. All findings are descriptive: four cases, two desi