onfigurations: the influence-guided configuration studied above, a configuration that aggregates rows into cells and solves a relaxed removal problem with exact swap polishing, and one that solves a stationarity linear program over target ratings. All three scored 0.4993, the same as the starting baseline (difference 0.0); all three were scored (3 of 3, none missing). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study]]\n203:Stationarity linear program & 0.4993 & 0.9987 & 0.0 & 0.5 & 114\\\\\n225:We measured how few comparisons must be deleted to reverse the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair in a Bradley--Terry leaderboard, using exact-refit verification, comparators and controls. On two held-out tables of real data, the verified budgets were 62 and 4 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 135,630-row crowd-vote table and 62 and 8 rows for the top and mid pairs of a 12,900-row sports table. Mid pairs were cheaper than top pairs, random deletion rarely helped, and label-permuted tables flipped with far fewer rows than real ones. A static first-order influence ranking matched the more elaborate search everywhere, and three search configurations tied on development tables, so we claim a measurement and not a better method. The crowd-versus-sports difference in percent of comparisons is mostly table size and is confounded with participants and era. An expert-judgment table on the development side did not flip within the cap, with unknown cause. Next steps that the evidence motivates are more tables per design, rank-interval events, exhaustive or exact solvers on small tables to test how far the certified budgets are from the true minimum, and adversarial perturbations beyond deletion.\n269:The Newton fit uses analytic gradient $\\nabla L=\\sum_i (p_i-s_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})+\\lambda\\theta$ and Hessian $H=\\sum_i w_i\\,x_ix_i^\\top+\\lambda I$, with step halving until the objective stops increasing (at most 60 iterations, gradient tolerance $10^{-9}$). Eq.~\\eqref{eq:infl