{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvoAdaq3VhstXvdUEnoM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Bc8U5zZjc6NwcrVx85JwoK","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","old_string":"We cannot separate these factors with two tables, and we therefore do not attribute the contrast to evaluation design.","new_string":"The choice of normaliser also matters. Dividing by the mean number of comparisons a participant appears in ($2N/M$, derived: 5,217 for the crowd table and 860 for the sports table) instead of by $N$ reverses the direction of the size effect: the 62-row top-pair budget is 1.2\\% of a crowd participant's comparisons and 7.2\\% of a team's, and the mid-pair budgets are 0.077\\% and 0.93\\%. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases.F1.studied_percent_of_mean_participant_comparisons]]\nNeither normaliser is privileged: the share of the table is the natural unit for an adversary or a noise process that perturbs a fixed fraction of all votes, whereas the share of a participant's own comparisons is closer to the evidence behind that participant's rating. Both views give the same qualitative picture (small budgets, mid cheaper than top), but they disagree about which table looks more fragile. We cannot separate these factors with two tables, and we therefore do not attribute the contrast to evaluation design."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1652,"cache_read_input_tokens":176134,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1652},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"69025aa5-9399-474f-82bf-b38cf111820f","uuid":"db980fd9-ce55-4ccf